  "image_name": "2ce4d96ea324e0d708de0089d285a423.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three ways a bivariate system can move closer to a threshold associated with an adverse event. In all panels, two variables (“Driver/hazard 1” and “Driver/hazard 2”) are shown as joint probability contours, with an upper-right shaded region indicating combinations that cross a critical boundary. Gray and red distributions represent two different states, likely before and after change.\n\nThe key message is that risk can rise even when the one-dimensional distributions change in different ways. In the first case, the red joint distribution shifts in mean toward the threshold, so more of the probability mass overlaps the dangerous region. In the second, the center stays more similar but the red contours spread out, showing increased variance; this broader distribution reaches the threshold more often. In the third, the marginal distributions along the top and right look nearly unchanged, but the red contours become narrower and more aligned diagonally, indicating stronger dependence between the two variables. That increased correlation alone pushes more joint probability into the critical region.\n\nOverall, the figure illustrates that changing means, variances, or dependence can each increase compound-event likelihood.",
